WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:06.130
Pixar's beautiful new Day of the Dead animation
Coco is full of easter eggs including teases

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:09.019
to Incredibles 2, Toy Story, and Finding Nemo.

00:00:09.019 --> 00:00:12.950
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I'm going to run through all the

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:17.630
Pixar easter eggs in Coco, some cool cameos
you might have missed, plus I'm explaining

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.260
how Coco fits into the Pixar theory.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:23.400
And I'm starting a new Disney Funko Pop giveaway
on this video.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.760
To enter, make sure you're a subscriber and
leave a comment about Coco.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.150
Towards the start of the movie, when Miguel
runs through the streets of his hometown,

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:36.519
if you look very carefully, you'll notice
some Pixar-themed piñatas on sale, including

00:00:36.519 --> 00:00:40.449
Woody and Buzz Lightyear from the Toy Story
movies and Mike from Monsters Inc.

00:00:40.449 --> 00:00:44.840
Which is especially fitting as Coco director
Lee Unkrich was also a director on two of

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.749
the Toy Story movies and Monsters, Inc.

00:00:47.749 --> 00:00:53.359
The Pizza Planet truck made its first appearance
back in 1995 in the very first Toy Story movie,

00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:57.140
and if you love spotting Pixar's easter egg
regular pop up in their films, then you'll

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:02.420
be glad to see it in Coco as it drives past
Miguel's house playing music.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.250
"No music!"

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:09.000
By the way, Pizza Planet is actually written
in Spanish on this truck because, according

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.590
to director Lee Unkrich, the idea is that
there's a Pizza Planet franchise in Mexico.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:18.070
Continuing the Toy Story theme, one of the
performers at the talent contest in the Land

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:22.750
of the Dead is wearing a skull T-shirt, just
like Sid wore in the first and third Toy Story

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.490
movies.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:27.990
Also in the Land of the Dead, you'll find
the Luxo ball on a table in the background

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.960
when Dante and Frida Kahlo's monkey race around
her workshop.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.710
The Luxo ball appeared in Pixar's very first
short film, Luxo Jr, together with two desk

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:40.340
lamps, one of which is still used in Pixar's
logo.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:44.881
And the Lucha Libre stand in Miguel's hometown
has numerous wrestling masks on sale, one

00:01:44.881 --> 00:01:48.700
of which has the same colour design as the
Luxo ball as well as its star.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:53.189
Fans of Finding Nemo and its sequel should
keep their eyes open for the curious little

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:55.979
clownfish and his dad who pop up in a couple
of places.

00:01:55.979 --> 00:02:00.830
First, there are Nemo, Marlin, and Dory figurines
on the alebrijes table in the town.

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:05.350
And I'm wondering if these figures could also
be Destiny from Finding Dory and if these

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:07.590
could be Remy from Ratatouille?

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:12.050
By the way, there's also a little sculpture
of the Coco character Pepita here, who turns

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:14.569
up later in the Land of the Dead.

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:18.550
And sneakily hidden among the marigolds on
the Rivera family's ofrenda, there's another

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:19.550
Nemo.

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:23.330
He's really hard to see as his colour blends
in with the flowers, but now you know where

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:25.610
he is, you should be able to spot him!

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:30.970
The A113 easter egg, which has appeared in
every single Pixar movie to date, can be found

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.610
on the door of the Bureau of Family Grievances
in the Land of the Dead.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:39.580
The appearance of A113 in Pixar's films is
an homage to the classroom at California Institute

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:43.220
of the Arts where many of Pixar's animators
studied.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:47.500
Pixar's films always include easter eggs for
their next movie, which in the case of Coco

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.280
means Incredibles 2.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.129
In the Land of the Dead, when Miguel and Héctor
are on their way to the talent contest, you

00:02:53.129 --> 00:02:57.230
need to look very carefully as, down one of
the streets, there's a hard-to-spot Incredibles

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:58.230
poster.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:01.610
You can see it when someone sets off a firework,
which lights up the wall of a building where

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:03.319
the poster is.

00:03:03.319 --> 00:03:08.280
Coco includes numerous cameos by icons of
Mexican culture and history in the Land of

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:09.280
the Dead.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.950
There's a funny moment in the queue to get
into Ernesto de la Cruz's party where the

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.770
bouncer gets a selfie of his disembodied skull
taken with El Santo.

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:21.450
El Santo was a legendary figure in Lucha Libre,
or Mexican professional wrestling, and was

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:25.180
known as "el Enmascarado de Plata" because
of the silver mask you can see him wearing

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.190
to the party in Coco.

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:32.099
The famous Mexican film stars Jorge Negrete
and Pedro Infante also appear at de la Cruz's

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:33.099
party.

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:38.099
In fact, the character of Ernesto de la Cruz
was actually partly inspired by Pedro Infante.

00:03:38.099 --> 00:03:42.549
Mexico's renowned comic film actor Cantinflas
appears later during a group sing-song with

00:03:42.549 --> 00:03:43.870
de la Cruz.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:49.060
Also in the group is the famed Mexican revolutionary
Emiliano Zapata and the influential Mexican

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:51.340
actress and singer, María Félix.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:56.150
The orchestra conductor at the big concert
towards the end of the movie is a nod by Pixar's

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.549
animators to Michael Giacchino, who composed
the score for Coco.

00:03:59.549 --> 00:04:03.610
The concert's conductor wears a bowtie and
top hat and his look reminded me of photos

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.019
like this one of Giacchino.

00:04:05.019 --> 00:04:11.310
In real life, Frida Kahlo was a hugely important
Mexican artist, and in Coco, she plays a suitably

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:15.450
significant and artistic role during Miguel's
journey in the Land of the Dead.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:20.139
Scattered around Kahlo's workshop in the film
are references to some of her real paintings,

00:04:20.139 --> 00:04:24.490
including her self-portraits and still life
works with watermelons, papayas, and coconuts,

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:27.610
the Spanish word for which just happends to
be 'coco'.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:31.759
And Frida's alebrije monkey and her affinity
with Miguel's dog Dante reflects the fact

00:04:31.759 --> 00:04:36.580
that, in real life, Frida Kahlo had several
pet monkeys as well as xolos, or Mexican hairless

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:38.250
dogs as they're also known.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:43.130
John Ratzenberger, Pixar's good-luck charm,
has voiced a character in all of Pixar's movies

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:46.560
so far, and in Coco he has a small role as
the voice of Juan Ortodoncia.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:53.660
The promo clip, Dante's Lunch, features a
cool Cars easter egg via the Number 95 shoes

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.840
a little boy is wearing as Dante zooms past
him.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:00.500
In the main film, there's also a possible
Lightning McQueen easter egg via the lightning

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:02.759
bolt on Miguel's T-shirt.

00:05:02.759 --> 00:05:07.180
By the way, there are three different Coco
easter eggs in Pixar's last movie, Cars 3,

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:10.720
and if you want to find those and all the
others you missed in that movie, hit the link

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.840
at the end of this video or in the video description
below.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.770
Ok, what about the Pixar theory and how does
Coco fit in?

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:21.490
For anyone who doesn't know, there are a number
of Pixar theories around, but the best-known

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:26.520
one is by Jon Negroni who has suggested a
unified timeline for all the Pixar films.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.979
In Coco, we can see real-life Pizza Planet
trucks driving around and Pixar characters

00:05:30.979 --> 00:05:34.850
appearing in piñatas and alebrijes which
does tie in with the theory that a shared

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:36.250
universe exists.

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:40.830
The Cars easter eggs though might be a little
problematic as, if we assume Coco takes place

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:45.040
in the present day, then there's a potential
timeline problem because, according to Negroni's

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:50.289
Pixar theory, the Cars movies don't take place
until some time after 2110 and the events

00:05:50.289 --> 00:05:53.039
of WALL-E.
However, the Pixar theory posits that the

00:05:53.039 --> 00:05:57.560
Cars characters were at one time driven by
real human owners and when they later came

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:01.509
alive they inherited the characteristics of
their previous human drivers.

00:06:01.509 --> 00:06:05.460
Which means Lightning McQueen might have had
a real human racing driver who was alive around

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:09.289
the time when Coco takes place, hence the
Cars easter eggs.

